  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "$\\int (1+2x+{tan}^{2}\u2061x)\\text{d}x=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we select the identity that contains a ${tan}^{2}\u2061x$ term. So we can use the identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}\u2061x={sec}^{2}\u2061x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1+2x+{tan}^{2}\u2061x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+{tan}^{2}\u2061x+2x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int ({sec}^{2}\u2061x+2x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x+{x}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
